<Docs
	page="recipes/java"
	title="Java"
	lede="JDBC and Spring's JdbcTemplate work out of the box, ? placeholders and all."
>
	<section>
		<h2>Which strings squill rewrites</h2>
		<p>
			Text blocks (<code>"""</code>, Java 15 and later). Java strips a text
			block&rsquo;s common indentation, so squill can lay the query out one
			clause per line without changing what the database sees beyond
			whitespace. Plain strings, and queries built up with <code>+</code>,
			are left exactly as written &mdash; to have squill format a query,
			write it as a text block. A text block holding an escape
			(<code>\n</code>, <code>\"</code>) is reported rather than rewritten.
		</p>
		<p>
			The built-in <code>java</code> grammar&rsquo;s default query finds a
			text block passed straight to a JDBC, JPA, or <code>JdbcTemplate</code>{" "}
			method. One assigned to a variable or a constant first is out of its
			sight. JDBC&rsquo;s <code>?</code> placeholders parse as parameters
			for this grammar without any configuration.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>JDBC</h2>
		<p>
			<code>prepareStatement</code>, <code>prepareCall</code>,{" "}
			<code>executeQuery</code>, <code>executeUpdate</code>,{" "}
			<code>execute</code>, and <code>addBatch</code>:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="java-jdbc" lang="java" />
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Spring JdbcTemplate</h2>
		<p>
			<code>query</code>, <code>queryForObject</code>,{" "}
			<code>queryForList</code>, <code>update</code>,{" "}
			<code>batchUpdate</code>, and the rest of the family. The single-line
			string at the end stays as written:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="java-spring" lang="java" />
		<div class="note">
			<p>
				Named parameters (<code>:customerId</code>, as{" "}
				<code>NamedParameterJdbcTemplate</code> and JPA use them) and
				JPA&rsquo;s numbered <code>?1</code> don&rsquo;t parse in the
				Postgres dialect yet. squill reports those queries and leaves them as
				written.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>
</Docs>
